This is a real bug and your root cause analysis is correct. The `i2_s` kernels are gated entirely on `#if defined(__AVX2__)` with no scalar fallback, so on AVX1-only hardware the unpacking silently produces zeros/garbage rather than falling back to correct scalar math.

The fix pattern that works: a runtime dispatch table selected at startup based on `cpuid` output, not compile-time defines. This way the same binary runs correctly on AVX1, AVX2, and AVX-512 hardware — each codepath is compiled separately and selected at runtime.

Something like:
```c
typedef void (*dot_fn)(float*, const void*, const int8_t*, int);
static dot_fn dispatch_ternary_dot(void) {
    if (__builtin_cpu_supports("avx512vnni")) return ternary_dot_avx512;
    if (__builtin_cpu_supports("avx2"))       return ternary_dot_avx2;
    return ternary_dot_scalar;  // always-correct fallback
}
```

We use this pattern in Project Zero (https://github.com/shifulegend/project-zero) — it handles AVX-512F, VNNI, VBMI, AVX2, and scalar paths in the same binary. The scalar fallback is verified to produce bit-identical output to the SIMD paths, which is the only way to catch regressions like this. The AVX2-only compile-time gating without a scalar fallback is a silent correctness trap on older hardware.
